      {
        "source_handle": "P0146",
        "title": "Gut microbiota shapes cancer immunotherapy responses",
        "year": "2025",
        "doi": "10.1038/s41522-025-00786-8",
        "paper_id": "4171288ecca93c6bca3eee61295418d704bb00fd",
        "material_depth": "fulltext",
        "material_status": "resolved",
        "aliases": [],
        "study_summary_A": {
          "approach": "As a narrative review, this paper does not employ a systematic search protocol described in the text. Instead, it synthesizes findings from a broad range of sources including: 1) Historical timeline analysis of key advancements; 2) Summary of clinical trial data (Tables 1-5) linking specific microbial taxa to ICI/ACT outcomes; 3) Mechanistic discussion based on preclinical models (mouse studies) and human cohort analyses regarding immune cell activation, metabolite pathways (SCFAs, bile acids, etc.), and intratumoral interactions; 4) Evaluation of intervention strategies (antibiotics, FMT, probiotics, diet, engineered microbes) by summarizing results from ongoing and completed clinical trials.",
          "contribution_and_limits": [
            {
              "contribution": "Provides a structured synthesis of clinical and preclinical evidence linking specific gut microbial taxa to ICI and ACT outcomes across multiple cancer types. It categorizes mechanisms into innate/adaptive immunity modulation, metabolite effects, and intratumoral interactions. It also catalogs numerous ongoing clinical trials for FMT, probiotics, and dietary interventions.",
              "limits": "As a narrative review, it lacks a systematic methodology for study selection, potentially introducing selection bias. Many findings are derived from small-scale clinical studies or animal models, limiting generalizability. The field faces significant heterogeneity in sequencing methods, sample handling, and lack of standardized protocols, making cross-study comparisons difficult. Causal relationships between specific microbes and clinical outcomes are often correlational rather than proven causal."
            }
          ],
          "key_findings": [
            {
              "conditions": "ICI therapy (anti-PD-1/PD-L1/CTLA-4)",
              "finding": "Specific gut microbial compositions correlate with ICI response. Beneficial taxa include Bifidobacterium, Akkermansia muciniphila, Ruminococcaceae, and Faecalibacterium prausnitzii, which are associated with improved survival and tumor regression in melanoma, NSCLC, and RCC. Conversely, antibiotic use generally reduces ICI efficacy."
            },
            {
              "conditions": "ACT therapy (CAR-T, TCR-T)",
              "finding": "Gut microbiota influences ACT efficacy and toxicity. Certain microbes (e.g., Ruminococcus, Bacteroides) are linked to CAR-T efficacy, while others may predict neurotoxicity or cytokine release syndrome (CRS). Microbial metabolites like SCFAs can enhance CD8+ T cell cytotoxicity."
            },
            {
              "conditions": "Mechanisms of Immune Modulation",
              "finding": "Microbiota modulate immunity via direct interaction with dendritic cells and macrophages (PRR activation), metabolite signaling (SCFAs, bile acids, tryptophan metabolites influencing T cell differentiation), and molecular mimicry (cross-reactive T cells). Intratumoral microbiota can remodel the TME, promoting TLS formation or inducing immunosuppression."
            },
            {
              "conditions": "Intervention Strategies",
              "finding": "FMT from ICI responders can overcome resistance in refractory melanoma patients. Probiotics (e.g., Clostridium butyricum CBM588) and dietary interventions (high-fiber, ketogenic) show promise in enhancing ICI response and reducing toxicity, though clinical evidence is still emerging."
            }
          ],
          "paper_kind": "review",
          "problem_or_question": "Despite the success of immunotherapies like ICIs and ACTs, patient responses vary widely due to primary or acquired resistance and adverse events. This review aims to elucidate the role of the gut microbiota in shaping these outcomes, identify potential biomarkers for predicting response, and explore microbiota-based strategies to enhance efficacy and mitigate toxicity.",
          "research_scope": "The review focuses on the gut microbiota and its influence on cancer immunotherapy, specifically immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICIs) and adoptive cell therapies (ACTs). It covers clinical correlations, preclinical mechanisms, intratumoral microbiota, and intervention strategies across various solid tumors and hematologic malignancies.",
          "work_summary": "This paper provides a comprehensive overview of how the gut microbiome modulates the efficacy and toxicity of cancer immunotherapies. It traces the historical evolution from early animal studies to recent clinical trials, detailing specific microbial signatures associated with response or resistance to ICIs and ACTs. The authors synthesize mechanistic insights involving innate and adaptive immune modulation, metabolite production, and tumor microenvironment remodeling. Furthermore, it evaluates therapeutic interventions such as fecal microbiota transplantation (FMT), probiotics, dietary modifications, and engineered bacteria, while highlighting challenges in standardization and clinical translation."
        },
        "review_planning_B": {
          "planning_summary": "This review serves as a foundational resource for mapping the relationship between gut microbiome composition and ICI efficacy in solid tumors. It provides specific microbial markers (taxa) associated with response/resistance, details mechanistic pathways (metabolites, immune cell crosstalk), and outlines current intervention strategies (FMT, probiotics, diet). It is particularly useful for identifying candidate biomarkers and understanding the biological plausibility of microbiome-mediated modulation, while also highlighting the methodological challenges and variability that complicate definitive conclusions.",
          "facet_contributions": [
            {
              "boundaries": "Associations are largely correlative from retrospective or small prospective cohorts. Specificity varies by cancer type and ICI agent. Lack of standardized detection methods limits direct comparison. Does not establish causality.",
              "contribution": "Identifies specific gut microbial taxa associated with ICI response or resistance in solid tumors. For example, Akkermansia muciniphila, Bifidobacterium, and Ruminococcaceae are linked to better outcomes in melanoma and NSCLC, while certain Proteobacteria or high abundance of Bacteroides intestinalis may correlate with resistance or toxicity. It also notes that beneficial taxa vary by ICI type (anti-PD-1 vs. anti-CTLA-4) and cancer type.",
              "facet_id": "F1",
              "possible_uses": "Can be used to list candidate microbial biomarkers for predicting ICI efficacy in solid tumors. Helps explain inter-patient variability in treatment response."
            },
            {
              "boundaries": "Many mechanisms are primarily demonstrated in mouse models (preclinical). Human validation is often indirect or observational. Some mechanisms (e.g., molecular mimicry) are proposed but require further confirmation in humans. Effects can be bidirectional (promoting or suppressing immunity depending on context).",
              "contribution": "Details mechanisms by which gut microbiota modulate ICI efficacy: 1) Direct immune modulation via PRR activation (TLR/NLR) leading to DC maturation and T-cell activation; 2) Metabolite-mediated regulation (SCFAs inhibiting HDAC, bile acids affecting Th17/Treg balance, tryptophan metabolites via AhR); 3) Molecular mimicry where bacterial antigens cross-react with tumor antigens; 4) Remodeling of the tumor microenvironment (TME) through intratumoral bacteria or systemic metabolites influencing tertiary lymphoid structures (TLS).",
              "facet_id": "F2",
              "possible_uses": "Provides mechanistic explanations for how the microbiome influences ICI response. Supports the biological rationale for microbiome-targeted interventions."
            }
          ],
          "broader_review_uses": [
            {
              "connection_to_review": "Contextual background on the evolution of microbiome-immunotherapy research",
              "material": "Historical perspective section tracing discoveries from 2007 to 2025, highlighting the shift from correlation to mechanistic understanding and clinical trials.",
              "purpose": "To provide a timeline of key advancements and justify the current interest in microbiome modulation as a critical factor in immunotherapy success."
            },
            {
              "connection_to_review": "Discussion of intervention strategies beyond just biomarkers",
              "material": "Sections on FMT, probiotics, antibiotics, diet, and engineered microbes, including tables of clinical trials.",
              "purpose": "To illustrate potential therapeutic avenues for overcoming ICI resistance and managing toxicity, providing examples of how microbiome modulation is being translated into clinical practice."
            },
            {
              "connection_to_review": "Challenges and limitations in the field",
              "material": "Section on 'Challenges and future directions' discussing technical variability, donor effects in FMT, host genetics, and lack of standardization.",
              "purpose": "To highlight methodological hurdles and confounding factors that must be considered when interpreting microbiome-ICI associations, explaining why consistent biomarkers are hard to identify."
            }
          ],
          "scope_interpretation_cautions": [
            "The review covers both gut and intratumoral microbiota; ensure distinction is maintained when applying findings to 'gut microbiome' specific facets.",
            "Findings on specific bacterial taxa are highly dependent on the cancer type (e.g., melanoma vs. NSCLC) and the specific ICI agent (anti-PD-1 vs. anti-CTLA-4); avoid over-generalizing across all solid tumors.",
            "Many mechanistic insights are derived from murine models; caution is needed when extrapolating to human physiology without explicit human validation mentioned in the text.",
            "Clinical trial data cited are often from early-phase (I/II) or small cohorts; results should not be interpreted as definitive proof of efficacy for interventions like FMT or probiotics."
          ],
          "topic_handles": [
            "gut microbiota composition and diversity",
            "immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICIs)",
            "solid tumors (melanoma, NSCLC, RCC, HCC, CRC)",
