          "outline_action": "Identify and retrieve full-text or registry data for actual Phase III randomized controlled trials of FMT or defined microbial consortia in oncology (e.g., TACITO, though noted as randomized, verify if Phase III; other ongoing/completed Phase III trials like those in melanoma or RCC). If no such trials are found in the literature base, explicitly state that no Phase III RCTs have yet confirmed efficacy, using this Phase IIa negative result to illustrate the current stage of evidence (pre-registration/early-phase focus).",
          "outline_action_recovered_by_policy": false,
          "remaining_gap": "The gap question asks for confirmation of completed large-scale Phase III randomized controlled trials confirming microbiome intervention efficacy. This study is explicitly identified as a 'single-arm phase IIa' trial. It is neither Phase III nor randomized. Therefore, it does not fulfill the success criterion of providing data from a completed Phase III RCT.",
          "remaining_gap_recovered_by_policy": false,
          "status": "unmet",
          "useful_material": "This study reports the results of the MITRIC trial (NCT05286294), a single-arm, phase IIa basket trial evaluating Fecal Microbiota Transplantation (FMT) from immune checkpoint inhibitor (ICI) responders in patients with advanced cancers progressing on ICIs. The trial enrolled 12 patients across melanoma, head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (HNSCC), renal cell carcinoma (RCC), and microsatellite instability-high pancreatic cancer. The co-primary endpoints were safety and objective response rate (ORR). The study found that FMT was safe and feasible but demonstrated limited clinical activity: no objective responses were observed (ORR 0%), median progression-free survival was 1.5 months, and median overall survival was 10.1 months. Clinical benefit (stable disease >6 months) was achieved in only one patient. The authors conclude that FMT does not support efficacy as a salvage therapy in this non-biomarker-driven, heavily pretreated population."
        },
